1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:05,050
1903: The Straits Dollar,
Singapore's First Money of Its Own

2
00:00:05,050 --> 00:00:08,241
On the afternoon of the
29th of January 1906,

3
00:00:08,321 --> 00:00:13,256
the Legislative Council of the
Straits Settlements fixed the value of

4
00:00:13,336 --> 00:00:17,625
the dollar at two shillings
and fourpence in British money.

5
00:00:17,625 --> 00:00:22,089
The next morning's Straits Times
led its fifth page with the news,

6
00:00:22,169 --> 00:00:25,876
under the headline "Government
tries to buy £100,000 at

7
00:00:25,956 --> 00:00:30,420
Saturday's Rate and then lowers
the Dollar", and a reader wrote in

8
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:34,700
that "a most painful sensation
has been caused in Singapore".

9
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:38,750
What the day settled lasted
far longer than the row.

10
00:00:38,750 --> 00:00:42,618
For the first time, Singapore
had a dollar of its own whose

11
00:00:42,698 --> 00:00:46,164
value did not rise and fall
with the price of silver,

12
00:00:46,244 --> 00:00:50,647
backed by reserves under a currency
board, and that idea of a fully

13
00:00:50,727 --> 00:00:54,675
backed currency still underpins
the Singapore dollar today.

14
00:00:54,675 --> 00:00:57,245
For most of the nineteenth century

15
00:00:57,325 --> 00:00:59,975
Singapore had no money of its own.

16
00:00:59,975 --> 00:01:03,265
Trade ran on silver
dollars from elsewhere,

17
00:01:03,345 --> 00:01:07,888
first Spanish and then Mexican,
as told in the posts on the

18
00:01:07,968 --> 00:01:12,200
Spanish dollar and on the
1867 transfer to Crown rule.

19
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:16,203
After 1867 the Straits
recognised several foreign

20
00:01:16,283 --> 00:01:20,620
silver dollars as legal
tender, and from 1895 Britain

21
00:01:20,700 --> 00:01:24,700
struck a trade dollar of
its own for the region.

22
00:01:24,700 --> 00:01:29,550
Banks issued their own notes,
which had to be redeemed in silver.

23
00:01:29,550 --> 00:01:33,875
A silver dollar was worth
whatever its silver would fetch.

24
00:01:33,875 --> 00:01:36,609
Britain, Singapore's main trading partner,

25
00:01:36,689 --> 00:01:40,427
was on the gold standard, and
through the late nineteenth

26
00:01:40,507 --> 00:01:44,125
century the price of silver
against gold fell steeply.

27
00:01:44,125 --> 00:01:48,378
According to One Hundred
Years of Singapore (1921),

28
00:01:48,458 --> 00:01:54,150
the dollar was worth about four
shillings and sixpence around 1870.

29
00:01:54,150 --> 00:01:58,503
By 1893 it averaged about
two shillings and sevenpence,

30
00:01:58,583 --> 00:02:02,212
having dropped nearly
tenpence in three years,

31
00:02:02,292 --> 00:02:06,725
and in 1902 it sank to about
one shilling and sixpence.

32
00:02:06,725 --> 00:02:10,076
For anyone in Singapore
paid in dollars but owing

33
00:02:10,156 --> 00:02:14,488
money in sterling, such as a
European clerk sending money home,

34
00:02:14,568 --> 00:02:17,499
or a company paying
for goods from Britain,

35
00:02:17,579 --> 00:02:21,150
the value of the dollar
was a constant uncertainty.

36
00:02:21,150 --> 00:02:25,627
The Singapore Chamber of Commerce,
led mainly by European firms,

37
00:02:25,707 --> 00:02:29,125
pressed for a dollar with
a fixed value in gold.

38
00:02:29,125 --> 00:02:31,636
In 1897 one of its committees

39
00:02:31,716 --> 00:02:35,200
recommended fixing it at two shillings.

40
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:38,750
The proposal stalled, and
nothing was done until 1902,

41
00:02:38,830 --> 00:02:41,574
when the dollar fell again and the Chamber

42
00:02:41,654 --> 00:02:45,150
asked the government to
look at the question afresh.

43
00:02:45,150 --> 00:02:47,425
Not everyone agreed.

44
00:02:47,425 --> 00:02:51,948
According to Song Ong Siang's
One Hundred Years' History of the

45
00:02:52,028 --> 00:02:56,332
Chinese in Singapore (1923),
when a committee of experts sat

46
00:02:56,412 --> 00:03:00,863
in London at the end of 1902 to
consider the Straits currency,

47
00:03:00,943 --> 00:03:05,400
Chinese and other Asian merchants
sent it a counter-petition.

48
00:03:05,400 --> 00:03:09,264
They argued that "on the whole
cheap dollars have contributed

49
00:03:09,344 --> 00:03:12,690
largely to stimulate the
development of the resources

50
00:03:12,770 --> 00:03:16,892
of these Settlements", and would
go on doing so as long as China,

51
00:03:16,972 --> 00:03:20,075
the region's great trading
partner, used silver.

52
00:03:20,075 --> 00:03:23,945
The merchant Tan Keong
Saik had made the case

53
00:03:24,025 --> 00:03:27,800
against a fixed rate
in an article in 1897.

54
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:32,086
Many traders who had signed the
petition for a gold currency had

55
00:03:32,166 --> 00:03:36,600
since changed their minds and
asked to join the counter-petition.

56
00:03:36,600 --> 00:03:39,750
The groundwork had already been laid.

57
00:03:39,750 --> 00:03:44,285
In 1897 the Straits Settlements
set up a Board of Commissioners

58
00:03:44,365 --> 00:03:47,729
of Currency with the sole
right to issue notes,

59
00:03:47,809 --> 00:03:51,905
and from 1899 it began issuing
government currency notes,

60
00:03:51,985 --> 00:03:56,600
while the banks' right to issue
their own notes was wound down.

61
00:03:56,600 --> 00:04:00,300
The London committee,
known after its chairman,

62
00:04:00,380 --> 00:04:03,275
Sir David Barbour, reported in 1903.

63
00:04:03,275 --> 00:04:06,980
It recommended a new Straits
dollar of the same weight and

64
00:04:07,060 --> 00:04:10,830
fineness as the British trade
dollar, the withdrawal of the

65
00:04:10,910 --> 00:04:15,399
Mexican and British dollars once
enough new coins were in circulation,

66
00:04:15,479 --> 00:04:18,350
and, later, a fixed
value against the pound.

67
00:04:18,350 --> 00:04:23,363
On the 29th of May 1903 the Colonial
Secretary moved the adoption

68
00:04:23,443 --> 00:04:28,300
of the committee's recommendations
in the Legislative Council.

69
00:04:28,300 --> 00:04:32,675
Tan Jiak Kim, who sat on the
Council, said that since the Chinese

70
00:04:32,755 --> 00:04:37,403
counter-petition had been considered
in London, the Chinese would now

71
00:04:37,483 --> 00:04:42,075
accept its conclusions and do all
they could to support the scheme.

72
00:04:42,075 --> 00:04:45,543
In October 1903 the new Straits dollars,

73
00:04:45,623 --> 00:04:48,550
minted in India, began to arrive.

74
00:04:48,550 --> 00:04:50,914
Within a month, according to Song,

75
00:04:50,994 --> 00:04:53,725
counterfeits were already circulating.

76
00:04:53,725 --> 00:04:57,356
By November 1904 the
recoinage was complete,

77
00:04:57,436 --> 00:05:02,075
and some 35 million new
dollars had reached the colony.

78
00:05:02,075 --> 00:05:05,258
The British, Hong Kong
and Mexican dollars had

79
00:05:05,338 --> 00:05:08,600
stopped being legal
tender two months earlier.

80
00:05:08,600 --> 00:05:10,351
With the new coin in place,

81
00:05:10,431 --> 00:05:14,500
the government waited for the
right moment to fix its value.

82
00:05:14,500 --> 00:05:19,876
That came on the 29th of January 1906,
under Governor Sir John Anderson,

83
00:05:19,956 --> 00:05:24,275
when the dollar was fixed at
two shillings and fourpence.

84
00:05:24,275 --> 00:05:28,106
The Straits Times reported
the next day that, on the very

85
00:05:28,186 --> 00:05:32,360
afternoon the rate was being
decided, the government had tried

86
00:05:32,440 --> 00:05:35,585
to buy £100,000 in sterling
at the market rate,

87
00:05:35,665 --> 00:05:40,400
which was five-eighths of a penny
higher than the rate it then fixed.

88
00:05:40,400 --> 00:05:43,632
The banks refused to
sell, and the paper called

89
00:05:43,712 --> 00:05:47,025
the episode "an extraordinary
tour de finance".

90
00:05:47,025 --> 00:05:50,750
The system worked through
the Currency Commissioners.

91
00:05:50,750 --> 00:05:55,221
From 1906 they issued dollar
notes in Singapore in exchange for

92
00:05:55,301 --> 00:05:59,627
gold at a rate of $60 for £7,
and later in the year they were

93
00:05:59,707 --> 00:06:04,178
also allowed to pay out gold in
exchange for notes, so that the

94
00:06:04,258 --> 00:06:09,025
dollar could be turned into sterling,
and back, at the fixed rate.

95
00:06:09,025 --> 00:06:12,132
The notes were backed
by reserves of silver,

96
00:06:12,212 --> 00:06:15,400
gold and investments
held largely in London.

97
00:06:15,400 --> 00:06:19,396
By 1921, according to One
Hundred Years of Singapore,

98
00:06:19,476 --> 00:06:23,702
the law required the reserve
behind the notes to be held

99
00:06:23,782 --> 00:06:28,550
one-sixth in silver, one-third
in gold and half in securities.

100
00:06:28,550 --> 00:06:31,400
The troubles were not quite over.

101
00:06:31,400 --> 00:06:35,036
In 1906 the price of silver
rose so high that the

102
00:06:35,116 --> 00:06:39,813
silver in a Straits dollar was
worth more than its fixed value,

103
00:06:39,893 --> 00:06:42,850
and the coins risked being melted down.

104
00:06:42,850 --> 00:06:46,833
The government responded by
striking a smaller dollar,

105
00:06:46,913 --> 00:06:50,675
with the same fineness but
less silver, from 1907.

106
00:06:50,675 --> 00:06:55,483
At the end of 1907 there was a
run on the Currency Commissioners,

107
00:06:55,563 --> 00:06:59,775
and their gold reserve in
Singapore was quickly used up.

108
00:06:59,775 --> 00:07:03,650
The rate itself was
also debated for years.

109
00:07:03,650 --> 00:07:07,253
Some argued that two shillings
and fourpence was too high

110
00:07:07,333 --> 00:07:11,130
and hurt exporters of Straits
produce, while others credited

111
00:07:11,210 --> 00:07:15,201
the fixed rate with bringing in
capital and, because the dollar

112
00:07:15,281 --> 00:07:18,900
was worth more, attracting
the labour the region needed.

113
00:07:18,900 --> 00:07:22,752
The Straits dollar lasted
until the first Malayan

114
00:07:22,832 --> 00:07:26,844
dollar coins appeared in
1939, issued by a currency

115
00:07:26,924 --> 00:07:30,775
board that now covered
the Malay states as well.

116
00:07:30,775 --> 00:07:35,528
After separation from Malaysia,
Singapore set up its own Board

117
00:07:35,608 --> 00:07:39,738
of Commissioners of Currency
on the 12th of June 1967,

118
00:07:39,818 --> 00:07:45,275
chaired by the Minister for Finance,
Lim Kim San, as told in his post.

119
00:07:45,275 --> 00:07:48,400
The new board kept the old principle.

120
00:07:48,400 --> 00:07:52,207
According to the Ministry
of Finance, the currency in

121
00:07:52,287 --> 00:07:56,313
circulation was fully backed
by gold and foreign assets,

122
00:07:56,393 --> 00:08:00,127
and when convertibility on
demand was ended in 1982,

123
00:08:00,207 --> 00:08:04,900
that full backing was kept as a
requirement of the Currency Act.

124
00:08:04,900 --> 00:08:08,987
On the 1st of October 2002
the Board of Commissioners of

125
00:08:09,067 --> 00:08:13,229
Currency merged into the
Monetary Authority of Singapore,

126
00:08:13,309 --> 00:08:17,550
which became the issuer of
the country's notes and coins.

127
00:08:17,550 --> 00:08:20,824
The Currency Act was
retained, and with it the

128
00:08:20,904 --> 00:08:24,543
rule that the currency in
circulation must be fully

129
00:08:24,623 --> 00:08:28,050
backed by gold and foreign
assets at all times.

130
00:08:28,050 --> 00:08:32,716
Since 1967, Singapore's notes
have also been exchangeable at

131
00:08:32,796 --> 00:08:37,700
par with those of Brunei, an
arrangement that continues today.

132
00:08:37,700 --> 00:08:41,145
The details have changed
many times since 1906,

133
00:08:41,225 --> 00:08:44,295
and the dollar no longer has a fixed value

134
00:08:44,375 --> 00:08:47,900
against the pound or any
other single currency.

135
00:08:47,900 --> 00:08:50,869
But the basic promise
of the currency board,

136
00:08:50,949 --> 00:08:54,125
that every dollar issued
is matched by reserves

137
00:08:54,205 --> 00:08:57,600
held behind it, is the
one Singapore still keeps.

138
00:08:57,600 --> 00:09:01,435
Why it matters today: The
Straits dollar is remembered

139
00:09:01,515 --> 00:09:05,422
mostly by coin collectors,
yet the arguments of 1897 to

140
00:09:05,502 --> 00:09:09,555
1906 over silver, gold and
what backs a currency produced

141
00:09:09,635 --> 00:09:12,745
the system whose central
rule, full backing,

142
00:09:12,825 --> 00:09:17,175
still stands behind the money
in every Singaporean's pocket.
